.docname {Getting started}
.include {docs}

## Requirements

The project needs a stable Rust toolchain with support for edition 2024, which
ships with Rust 1.85 and later. Everything else is fetched by Cargo.

The GUI additionally needs a working graphics stack. On Linux that means Bevy's
usual development libraries:

```sh
sudo apt-get install -y \
    libgl1-mesa-dev libudev-dev libwayland-dev libx11-dev \
    libxcursor-dev libxi-dev libxkbcommon-dev libxrandr-dev pkg-config
```

## Get the source

```sh
git clone https://github.com/andreashdez/creature_life_cycle.git
cd creature_life_cycle
```

## Build

```sh
cargo build             # debug build, GUI included
cargo build --release   # optimized build
```

### Building without Bevy

Bevy is a game engine and brings a large dependency tree, which makes the first
build slow. It is therefore optional, behind the default-on `gui` feature, and
only the GUI needs it.

```sh
cargo build --no-default-features   # library, CLI, benches and tests only
```

Without default features the build pulls 49 crates instead of the full tree.
The `gui` binary declares `required-features = ["gui"]`, so asking for it
without the feature reports that rather than failing to compile.

| Feature | Default | Enables |
|---------|---------|---------|
| `gui` | on | The `bevy` dependency and the `gui` binary |

## Run

Run the CLI with the configuration file. If the file is absent, the program
creates it from built-in defaults first:

```sh
cargo run
```

Run a short, fully deterministic simulation:

```sh
cargo run -- --turns 10 --delay-ms 0 --seed 42
```

Run the visual explorer. It starts from fixed seed `42` by default, and the
first build compiles Bevy, so expect a wait:

```sh
cargo run --bin gui
```

> Note: Both front ends read and write the same configuration file. See
> [Configuration](configuration.qd) for its location and contents.

## Package the GUI for macOS

```sh
sh scripts/package_gui.sh
```

The script builds `--release`, assembles an app bundle, and zips it:

.filetree
    - target
      - package
        - **Creature Life Cycle.app**
        - **creature_life_cycle_gui-macos.zip**

Sprites and shaders are embedded into the executable at build time, so a
packaged build needs no asset directory beside it.

